window.WEBVERSE_UNITS = [{ "title": "Internet & WWW", "desc": "The network, standards and infrastructure that make the web work.", "topics": [["Internet", "The Internet is a global network of interconnected computer networks that exchange information using standard communication protocols.", "It connects private, public, academic and business networks around the world. Devices communicate through routers and network links. Data is divided into packets, addressed with IP, and delivered using protocols such as TCP and UDP. The Internet is the infrastructure on which services such as the Web, email and file transfer operate.", "A device connects to a local network, reaches an Internet service provider, and exchanges packets with remote servers. Routers forward packets towards their destination; transport protocols help applications communicate reliably or quickly.", "Wi-Fi at home connects a laptop to the Internet, allowing it to reach a university portal hosted elsewhere.", "Global connectivity; quick information sharing; supports many services.", "Requires infrastructure and connectivity; security and privacy risks exist.", "Internet is a network of networks. TCP/IP is the core protocol suite. The Internet is not the same thing as the Web.", null, "internet"], ["World Wide Web", "The World Wide Web is a system of interlinked documents and resources that users access over the Internet, commonly through browsers.", "The Web uses URLs to identify resources, HTTP or HTTPS to transfer them, and HTML to structure many pages. A browser requests a page from a web server and renders the returned content. Hyperlinks connect resources across sites.", "A user enters a URL; DNS helps locate the server; the browser sends an HTTP request; the server returns a response; the browser renders HTML, CSS and JavaScript.", "Opening a college website and following a link to its timetable is using the Web.", "Easy access to linked resources; supports multimedia and collaboration.", "Web resources may be inaccurate; sites can be unavailable or unsafe.", "WWW was proposed by Tim Berners-Lee. A website is accessed using a browser and web protocols.", null, "web"], ["HTTP", "HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol used to transfer web resources between clients and servers.", "HTTP uses a request-response model. A request includes a method, path, headers and optionally a body. The server sends a status code, headers and a response body. HTTP is stateless; cookies and sessions are commonly used when an application needs to remember a user. HTTPS protects HTTP traffic with TLS.", "Browser creates a request → server processes it → server returns a response. GET reads data, POST submits data, PUT replaces data, and DELETE removes data. Common status codes include 200, 301, 404 and 500.", "A browser sends a GET request when a student opens a course page.", "Simple and widely supported; supports different media types; extensible through headers.", "Plain HTTP does not encrypt traffic; stateless operation needs extra mechanisms for sessions.", "HTTP is an application-layer protocol. HTTPS = HTTP over TLS. Know request methods and status codes.", "<pre>GET /notes HTTP/1.1\\nHost: college.example\\n\\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK</pre>", "http"], ["SMTP", "SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) is used to send and relay email messages between mail clients and mail servers.", "An email application submits outgoing mail to an SMTP server. The sending server looks up the recipient domain's mail server and relays the message. SMTP handles sending; separate protocols such as POP3 or IMAP are commonly used to retrieve email.", "The sender composes a message → SMTP client submits it → sender server finds recipient mail server → message is relayed → recipient mailbox receives it.", "Pressing Send in an email application uses SMTP to submit outgoing mail.", "Standardised and widely supported; supports mail relaying.", "Delivery can be delayed; spam prevention and authentication require additional controls.", "SMTP is for sending email. DNS MX records help locate recipient mail servers.", null, "smtp"], ["POP3", "POP3 (Post Office Protocol version 3) is a protocol that allows an email client to retrieve messages from a mail server.", "The client connects to the mail server, authenticates, lists messages and downloads them. Depending on configuration, messages may be deleted from the server after download or retained. POP3 is straightforward but does not provide the same synchronisation experience as IMAP.", "Connect → authenticate → list messages → retrieve messages → optionally delete from server → disconnect.", "A desktop email program downloads mail so it can be read offline.", "Simple retrieval; messages can be read offline after download.", "Mailbox synchronisation across many devices is limited; folders and server state are basic.", "POP3 retrieves email. It commonly uses TCP port 110; secure POP3 commonly uses 995.", null, "pop3"], ["MIME", "MIME (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) extends email formats so messages can carry different content types and attachments.", "MIME labels the type and encoding of message content. It supports multipart messages containing text, images, documents, audio and other data. HTTP also uses MIME media types in Content-Type headers.", "A sender packages message parts with MIME headers; the receiver reads the media type and interprets each part appropriately.", "An email with a PDF attachment includes a MIME part identifying it as an application/pdf file.", "Supports attachments and rich content; standard media-type labels help interoperability.", "Different clients may render some content differently; unsafe attachments need scanning.", "MIME enables non-ASCII text, attachments and multipart email. Content-Type describes the media type.", null, "mime"], ["IMAP", "IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol) lets an email client access and manage messages stored on a mail server.", "Unlike the traditional download-oriented POP3 model, IMAP keeps mailbox state on the server and synchronises folders, read status and message changes between clients. This is useful when a person uses the same account on many devices.", "Client connects and authenticates → selects a mailbox → lists and fetches messages → synchronises flags and folders with the server.", "When an email is marked read on a phone, the laptop can show it as read too.", "Excellent multi-device synchronisation; server-side folders and search.", "Needs network access for full mailbox operations; server storage is used.", "IMAP is for accessing and synchronising mail. Common ports are 143 and secure 993.", null, "imap"], ["DNS", "DNS (Domain Name System) is a distributed naming system that translates domain names into information such as IP addresses.", "People remember names more easily than numeric IP addresses. A DNS resolver searches a hierarchy of DNS servers and records. A records map names to IPv4 addresses, AAAA to IPv6, CNAME creates aliases, and MX identifies mail servers. Caching reduces repeated lookup time.", "Browser asks resolver → resolver checks cache → queries DNS hierarchy when needed → receives record → browser connects to the returned IP address.", "DNS helps a browser find the server for learn.example.edu.", "Human-readable naming; distributed and scalable; caching improves speed.", "Misconfiguration can make services unreachable; DNS spoofing and cache poisoning are security concerns.", "DNS uses a hierarchical distributed architecture. Know A, AAAA, CNAME, MX and caching.", "<svg viewBox='0 0 620 160' class='diagram'><rect x='10' y='48' width='150' height='62' rx='10'/><rect x='235' y='48' width='150' height='62' rx='10'/><rect x='460' y='48' width='150' height='62' rx='10'/><g><text x='85' y='74'>Browser</text><text x='85' y='94'>asks for a name</text><text x='310' y='74'>DNS Resolver</text><text x='310' y='94'>finds a record</text><text x='535' y='74'>IP Address</text><text x='535' y='94'>server location</text></g><path d='M160 79 H230 M385 79 H455' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2' fill='none'/><path d='m222 72 8 7-8 7 M447 72 455 79 447 86' stroke='currentColor' fill='none' stroke-width='2'/></svg>", "dns"], ["Web Browsers", "A web browser is software that requests, interprets and displays web resources for users.", "Browsers handle URLs, network requests, HTML parsing, CSS styling, JavaScript execution and page rendering. They also provide bookmarks, history, caching, developer tools and security controls. Rendering engines turn document structure and styles into pixels on screen.", "The browser resolves a URL, requests resources, parses HTML into the DOM, parses CSS into style rules, runs scripts, calculates layout and paints the page.", "Chrome, Firefox, Edge and Safari are browsers used to access learning portals.", "User-friendly access to web content; supports rich media and interactive pages.", "Different browsers may behave differently; unsafe extensions or sites can create risks.", "Browser is a client application. HTML structures content, CSS styles it and JavaScript adds behaviour.", null, "browser"], ["Web Servers", "A web server is software, and the computer or environment running it, that receives web requests and returns resources or application responses.", "A server listens for HTTP or HTTPS requests. It can return static files such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, or forward requests to application code to generate dynamic responses. Servers also manage logging, access and caching.", "Listen for request → interpret URL and method → locate or generate resource → send status, headers and response body.", "A university web server responds when students open the examination timetable page.", "Centralised content delivery; serves many users; can host dynamic applications.", "Needs maintenance and security updates; heavy traffic can overload a server.", "Client-server model: browser is the client; web server provides the requested resource.", null, "server"]] }, { "title": "HTML, CSS, JavaScript & XML", "desc": "The building blocks used to structure, present and make web documents interactive.", "topics": [["HTML", "HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to structure web page content.", "HTML uses elements and attributes to describe headings, paragraphs, links, images, forms, lists, tables and sections. Browsers parse HTML into a document tree. Semantic tags such as header, nav, main, article and footer communicate the role of each part.", "Write elements in an HTML document → browser parses tags → creates DOM nodes → displays structured content.", "A course page uses headings for units, paragraphs for notes and links for navigation.", "Simple to learn; supported by all modern browsers; semantic elements improve accessibility.", "HTML alone does not provide complex styling or application logic.", "Use <!doctype html>, html, head and body. Elements may have attributes. Prefer semantic HTML.", "<pre>&lt;h1&gt;Web Technology&lt;/h1&gt;\\n&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the course.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>", "html"], ["Lists", "HTML lists group related items into ordered, unordered or description lists.", "An unordered list uses ul with li items. An ordered list uses ol and li, optionally starting from a chosen number. A description list uses dl, dt and dd for terms and their descriptions. Lists can be nested when information has levels.", "Select list type → place each item in li (or dt/dd for description lists) → browser renders markers and indentation.", "A syllabus can use an ordered list for steps and an unordered list for topics.", "Clear grouping; native semantics help screen readers; easy to style with CSS.", "Deeply nested lists may become difficult to read; default markers may need styling.", "ul is unordered, ol is ordered, li is a list item, dl is a description list.", "<pre>&lt;ol&gt;\\n  &lt;li&gt;Learn HTML&lt;/li&gt;\\n  &lt;li&gt;Practise CSS&lt;/li&gt;\\n&lt;/ol&gt;</pre>", "lists"], ["Tables", "An HTML table presents related data in rows and columns.", "A table uses table, tr for rows, th for header cells and td for data cells. Caption describes the table. thead, tbody and tfoot group sections. Tables should represent tabular data, not be used for general page layout.", "Browser reads rows and cells → associates headers where marked → displays a grid of data.", "A timetable shows days in rows and lecture periods in columns.", "Good for structured comparisons; header cells improve accessibility.", "Wide tables can be hard to view on phones; complex tables need correct header associations.", "Use th for headings, td for data, caption for a title and scope for header direction.", "<pre>&lt;table&gt;\\n &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Unit&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Hours&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\\n &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;7&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\\n&lt;/table&gt;</pre>", "table"], ["Images", "The HTML img element embeds an image resource in a web page.", "The src attribute identifies the image and alt provides alternative text describing meaningful content. Width and height help reserve layout space. Modern HTML supports responsive sources through picture and srcset. Decorative images should have empty alt text.", "Browser reads img source → downloads image → decodes it → paints it within the page layout.", "A lesson page displays a labelled diagram of the client-server model.", "Adds visual explanation; alt text provides an accessible alternative; responsive images can save bandwidth.", "Large images slow pages; missing alt text harms accessibility; external images may break.", "Use meaningful alt text; specify dimensions; use appropriate image formats and responsive sources.", "<pre>&lt;img src=\"network.png\" alt=\"A client connected to a web server\" width=\"500\"&gt;</pre>", "image"], ["Forms", "HTML forms collect information from users through controls such as inputs, selects, textareas and buttons.", "A form groups controls and submits their name-value pairs to a destination. Labels identify controls. Input types provide suitable keyboards and validation. JavaScript can enhance validation, but server-side validation is required in real applications.", "User fills controls → browser checks built-in constraints → submit event occurs → data is sent using the chosen method when a destination exists.", "A student fills a contact form with name, email and feedback.", "Native validation; many built-in controls; accessible labels improve usability.", "Poorly designed forms confuse users; client-side validation alone is not secure.", "Understand form, action, method, label, name, required and input types.", "<pre>&lt;form&gt;\\n &lt;label for=\"email\"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;\\n &lt;input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" required&gt;\\n &lt;button&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\\n&lt;/form&gt;</pre>", "form"], ["Frames", "Frames divide a browser view into separate document areas; the older frameset/frame elements are obsolete in HTML5.", "Older websites used frameset and frame to display multiple documents in one window. Modern HTML5 uses iframe to embed another browsing context, for example a map or video. Embeds should have a descriptive title and be configured carefully for security and privacy.", "An iframe loads a separate document inside the current page. The embedded document has its own browsing context and may have sandbox restrictions.", "A page embeds a map or tutorial video using an iframe.", "Can embed another document or service; useful for isolated content.", "Can affect accessibility and mobile layout; embedded content can introduce security and privacy risks; legacy frames are obsolete.", "HTML5 removed frameset and frame. iframe remains supported; add title and consider sandbox and allow attributes.", "<pre>&lt;iframe src=\"https://example.com\" title=\"Example content\" sandbox&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</pre>", "frames"], ["JavaScript", "JavaScript is a programming language that adds logic and interactivity to web pages.", "The browser executes JavaScript to respond to events, validate user input, calculate values and update the DOM. JavaScript supports variables, functions, conditions, loops, arrays, objects and asynchronous operations. Scripts can be placed in a separate file and loaded with defer.", "Browser loads script → executes statements → registers event listeners → reacts to user actions → updates the page.", "A button click reveals a hidden solution or checks a quiz answer.", "Makes pages interactive; runs in the browser without a separate installation; large ecosystem.", "Incorrect scripts can cause errors; untrusted scripts can be dangerous; browser differences require testing.", "JavaScript is not Java. Learn variables, functions, DOM selection, events and conditionals.", "<pre>document.querySelector('button')\\n .addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {\\n  alert('Hello, student!');\\n });</pre>", "javascript"], ["DHTML", "DHTML (Dynamic HTML) describes the use of HTML, CSS, JavaScript and the DOM together to make a page change after loading.", "DHTML is not a separate programming language. HTML provides structure, CSS controls presentation, JavaScript handles events and logic, and the DOM exposes page elements as objects. Together they can show menus, update content and animate elements without a full page reload.", "An event occurs → JavaScript handles it → script changes DOM content or CSS properties → browser recalculates and redraws the affected part.", "A collapsible course menu opens when a student presses its heading.", "Provides interactive page behaviour; can update parts of the page quickly.", "Too many effects can distract or slow the page; scripts must support keyboard and assistive technologies.", "DHTML = HTML + CSS + JavaScript + DOM. It makes pages dynamic.", "<pre>document.getElementById('note').textContent = 'Lesson complete!';</pre>", "dhtml"], ["CSS", "CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is a style sheet language used to control the appearance and layout of HTML documents.", "CSS rules contain selectors and declarations. The cascade decides which declaration applies by origin, importance, specificity and source order. CSS supports typography, colours, spacing, borders, Grid, Flexbox, transitions and responsive media queries.", "Browser parses CSS → matches selectors to DOM elements → calculates computed styles → uses styles during layout and painting.", "A stylesheet makes all unit headings blue and gives cards rounded corners.", "Separates content from presentation; reusable rules keep design consistent; responsive layouts support many screens.", "Specificity can be confusing; poorly optimised styles can cause visual or performance issues.", "Know selector, property, value, class, id, cascade, box model, Flexbox, Grid and media queries.", "<pre>.card {\\n  background: #fff;\\n  padding: 1rem;\\n  border-radius: 12px;\\n}</pre>", "css"], ["XML", "XML (Extensible Markup Language) is a text-based markup format for storing and transporting structured data.", "Unlike HTML, XML lets authors define meaningful tags for their data. XML documents must be well-formed: elements are properly nested, tags are closed and there is one root element. XML is case-sensitive and supports attributes and namespaces.", "Create a tree of named elements → encode values as text or attributes → send or store document → processor parses and validates it if a schema is provided.", "A college system exchanges student details as XML between applications.", "Platform-independent text format; self-descriptive tags; supports structured exchange.", "Can be verbose; parsing and schema management add complexity.", "XML is case-sensitive. One root element is required. Tags must be properly nested and closed.", "<pre>&lt;student&gt;\\n &lt;name&gt;Asha&lt;/name&gt;\\n &lt;roll&gt;24&lt;/roll&gt;\\n&lt;/student&gt;</pre>", "xml"], ["DTD", "A Document Type Definition (DTD) defines the legal structure and permitted elements and attributes of an XML document.", "A DTD can declare elements, attributes, entities and relationships between elements. It can be internal within an XML document or external in a separate file. It helps a parser check whether the document follows a declared grammar.", "Declare permitted elements and attributes → associate the DTD with XML → validate the XML structure against the declarations.", "A DTD may specify that a book contains a title followed by one or more chapters.", "Defines document structure; supports consistency between documents.", "Limited data types compared with XML Schema; XML namespace support is limited.", "DTD defines structure, not visual styling. Internal and external DTDs are possible.", "<pre>&lt;!ELEMENT student (name, roll)&gt;\\n&lt;!ELEMENT name (#PCDATA)&gt;\\n&lt;!ELEMENT roll (#PCDATA)&gt;</pre>", "dtd"], ["XML Schema", "XML Schema (XSD) defines the permitted structure, elements, attributes and data types of XML documents.", "XSD can specify simple and complex types, required fields, element order, occurrence limits and restrictions such as numeric ranges. XML Schema is itself written in XML and provides stronger data typing than DTD.", "Create an XSD → reference it from the XML document or validation process → XML validator checks structure and values → report validity.", "A student record schema can require a name and integer roll number.", "Rich data types; supports namespaces; detailed constraints and reusable types.", "More verbose and harder to learn than simple DTD declarations.", "XSD validates structure and data types. It supports simpleType and complexType.", "<pre>&lt;xs:element name=\"roll\" type=\"xs:integer\"/&gt;</pre>", "xsd"], ["XML Object Models", "An XML object model represents XML data as structured objects or nodes that software can inspect and manipulate.", "A processor may expose XML content as a tree of elements, attributes and text. Applications can navigate the tree, query values or build new nodes. DOM is a well-known tree-based document object model; event-based approaches such as SAX process a stream.", "Parse XML → create or emit node information → application traverses nodes or responds to parser events → read or transform data.", "A result-processing application reads student and marks elements from an XML file.", "Provides a structured programming interface; simplifies data access.", "Tree models may consume memory; different APIs have different programming styles.", "XML object models let programs work with data structurally rather than searching raw text.", "<pre>const xml = new DOMParser().parseFromString(\\n  '&lt;course&gt;&lt;title&gt;Web Tech&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/course&gt;',\\n  'application/xml'\\n);\\nconsole.log(xml.querySelector('title').textContent);</pre>", "xmlmodel"], ["DOM", "The Document Object Model (DOM) is a programming interface that represents a document as a tree of objects.", "Each HTML element, attribute and text section can be represented by a node. JavaScript can select nodes, change text, update attributes, create elements and respond to events. The DOM connects page structure to scripts.", "HTML is parsed → DOM tree is created → JavaScript selects a node → changes data or structure → browser updates the display.", "JavaScript changes a heading when the user selects a new topic.", "Allows dynamic content; standard browser interface; supports event-driven pages.", "Frequent large DOM updates can be expensive; incorrect manipulation can affect accessibility.", "DOM is tree-based. Know document, element, text node, parent, child and event.", "<svg viewBox='0 0 520 170' class='diagram'><rect x='195' y='8' width='130' height='40' rx='8'/><rect x='55' y='110' width='120' height='40' rx='8'/><rect x='200' y='110' width='120' height='40' rx='8'/><rect x='345' y='110' width='120' height='40' rx='8'/><g><text x='260' y='33'>document</text><text x='115' y='135'>head</text><text x='260' y='135'>body</text><text x='405' y='135'>footer</text></g><path d='M260 48 V78 H115 V108 M260 78 V108 M260 78 H405 V108' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2'/></svg><pre>document.querySelector('h1').textContent = 'Hello DOM';</pre>", "dom"], ["SAX", "SAX (Simple API for XML) is an event-driven method for reading XML documents sequentially.", "A SAX parser reads a document from beginning to end and reports events such as start element, character data and end element. The application handles each event as it arrives. It does not normally build and retain the complete document tree.", "Read token → emit parser event → application handles event → continue until end of file.", "A system processes a very large XML catalogue record by record without storing the full tree in memory.", "Low memory usage; suitable for large sequential documents.", "Forward-only processing; application must manage state; modifying document structure is not straightforward.", "DOM builds a tree and supports random access. SAX streams events and is memory efficient.", "<pre>startElement('student')\\ncharacters('Asha')\\nendElement('student')</pre>", "sax"]] }, { "title": "Web Services", "desc": "How software systems communicate and share capabilities over a network.", "topics": [["Web Services", "A web service is a software component that exposes functionality for other applications to use over a network.", "Web services allow applications written in different languages or running on different platforms to exchange data through agreed interfaces. A client sends a request to a service endpoint and receives a response. SOAP-based services and REST-style services are common approaches.", "Provider exposes an endpoint → consumer sends a request using an agreed format → service processes it → consumer receives a response.", "A shopping site calls a payment gateway service to authorise a card payment.", "Supports integration across platforms; reusable business functions; loose coupling.", "Network failure and latency; security and version compatibility need attention.", "Web services enable machine-to-machine communication through standard network interfaces.", null, "service"], ["UDDI", "UDDI (Universal Description, Discovery and Integration) is a registry model for publishing and discovering web services.", "A service provider can publish business and technical details about a service. A consumer can search a registry to discover a suitable service and obtain its description. UDDI is traditionally studied alongside SOAP and WSDL.", "Provider publishes service details → registry stores metadata → consumer searches registry → consumer obtains service description and endpoint.", "A company could publish a directory entry for its shipping status service.", "Supports discovery; separates service description from implementation.", "Registry information must be maintained; less common in many modern application designs.", "UDDI is associated with service discovery and registry. SOAP handles messaging; WSDL describes interfaces.", null, "uddi"], ["SOAP", "SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol) is an XML-based protocol for exchanging structured messages between applications.", "A SOAP message contains an Envelope and Body, with an optional Header. The envelope identifies the message structure; the body carries the request or response. SOAP messages can be transported over HTTP and other protocols.", "Client builds XML envelope → sends to service endpoint → service reads envelope and body → processes operation → returns SOAP response.", "A banking application sends a SOAP request to obtain an account statement.", "Formal message structure; extensible headers; supports enterprise messaging standards.", "XML messages can be verbose; implementation is more complex than lightweight formats.", "SOAP uses XML. Main parts are Envelope, Header (optional) and Body.", "<pre>&lt;soap:Envelope&gt;\\n &lt;soap:Body&gt;\\n  &lt;GetStatus&gt;&lt;Id&gt;42&lt;/Id&gt;&lt;/GetStatus&gt;\\n &lt;/soap:Body&gt;\\n&lt;/soap:Envelope&gt;</pre>", "soap"], ["WSDL", "WSDL (Web Services Description Language) is an XML-based language that describes the operations and interface of a web service.", "A WSDL document describes data types, messages, operations, bindings and service endpoints. A client can inspect the contract to understand what operations are offered and how requests and responses should be formatted.", "Service defines interface → publishes WSDL → client reads operations and message definitions → client creates a correctly formatted request.", "A weather service WSDL describes a GetForecast operation and its input and output.", "Provides a machine-readable contract; helps tools generate client code.", "Can be lengthy and difficult to read manually; contract changes need coordination.", "WSDL describes what a service offers and how to communicate with it. It does not itself execute operations.", null, "wsdl"], ["Web Service Architecture", "Web service architecture describes how service providers, consumers and service descriptions interact.", "A provider implements and hosts a service. A consumer invokes it through a network endpoint. The service description explains supported operations and message formats. Traditional explanations may include publish, find and bind roles, often associated with UDDI, WSDL and SOAP.", "Publish service description → consumer finds service → consumer binds to endpoint → exchange request and response messages.", "A university portal invokes a separate results service to fetch marks.", "Supports distributed integration; encourages reuse; different platforms can interoperate.", "Depends on network availability; security, monitoring and service contracts add complexity.", "Remember provider, consumer, service description, endpoint and message exchange.", "<svg viewBox='0 0 600 210' class='diagram'><rect x='15' y='65' width='150' height='64' rx='10'/><rect x='435' y='65' width='150' height='64' rx='10'/><rect x='225' y='5' width='150' height='55' rx='10'/><g><text x='90' y='94'>Service Provider</text><text x='510' y='94'>Service Consumer</text><text x='300' y='37'>Service Registry</text></g><path d='M165 75 L225 46 M375 46 L435 75 M435 120 L165 120' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2'/><g font-size='12'><text x='174' y='47'>publish</text><text x='387' y='47'>find</text><text x='300' y='151'>invoke / response</text></g></svg>", "architecture"], ["Web Service Deployment", "Web service deployment is the process of installing, configuring and exposing a service so client applications can use it.", "Deployment includes packaging service code, configuring a runtime, selecting an endpoint, setting authentication and permissions, connecting dependencies and monitoring service health. A deployed service should have a clear contract and versioning approach.", "Develop service → package application → configure host and endpoint → deploy → test requests → monitor and maintain.", "A university deploys an attendance service so its student app can retrieve attendance records.", "Makes functionality available to many applications; independent release and scaling are possible.", "Configuration errors can cause downtime; updates can break older clients if not managed carefully.", "Deployment concerns hosting, endpoint, configuration, security, testing and monitoring.", null, "deployment"], ["AJAX", "AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML) is a technique for exchanging data with a server in the background and updating part of a page.", "JavaScript sends an asynchronous request and handles the response without reloading the whole document. Despite the name, modern AJAX often uses JSON rather than XML. The Fetch API is a common browser method for such requests.", "User action → JavaScript sends request → server returns data → script processes response → DOM updates selected section.", "A search field loads matching suggestions while a student types.", "Improves responsiveness; avoids full-page reloads; updates only necessary content.", "Needs error handling; network delays still occur; cross-origin restrictions and security must be considered.", "AJAX means asynchronous web communication. It is a technique, not a language; JSON is commonly used.", "<svg viewBox='0 0 580 150' class='diagram'><rect x='8' y='40' width='150' height='60' rx='9'/><rect x='215' y='40' width='150' height='60' rx='9'/><rect x='422' y='40' width='150' height='60' rx='9'/><g><text x='83' y='75'>User / Browser</text><text x='290' y='75'>JavaScript</text><text x='497' y='75'>Web Server</text></g><path d='M158 58 H210 M365 58 H417 M417 84 H365 M210 84 H158' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2'/><g font-size='11'><text x='184' y='48'>event</text><text x='389' y='48'>request</text><text x='390' y='101'>data</text></g></svg><pre>fetch('/data.json')\\n .then(r =&gt; r.json())\\n .then(data =&gt; console.log(data));</pre>", "ajax"], ["CORBA", "CORBA (Common Object Request Broker Architecture) is a standard that enables distributed objects to communicate across networks and programming languages.", "An Object Request Broker (ORB) manages requests between a client and a remote object. Interface definitions describe operations, so a client can invoke remote functionality without knowing the implementation language or location.", "Client invokes an object method → ORB locates remote object → request is transmitted → object executes operation → result returns through ORB.", "A legacy enterprise application calls a remote object implemented in a different programming language.", "Language and location independence; standardised distributed-object model.", "Complex infrastructure; configuration and interoperability can be challenging.", "ORB is central to CORBA. IDL describes object interfaces.", null, "corba"]] }, { "title": "Web 2.0 & Web 3.0", "desc": "From participatory websites to semantic, connected and data-driven experiences.", "topics": [["Web 2.0", "Web 2.0 describes the shift towards interactive websites where users create, share and collaborate on content.", "Instead of only reading static pages, users publish posts, edit wikis, rate products, share photographs and participate in communities. Web 2.0 commonly uses JavaScript, AJAX, RSS, APIs and collaborative platforms.", "Website provides tools → users contribute or interact → content and feedback are shared → community activity improves the service.", "A class wiki lets students collaboratively edit study notes.", "Encourages participation; fresh user-generated content; collaboration and sharing.", "Misinformation, privacy concerns, moderation burden and platform dependence.", "Web 2.0 is associated with participation, collaboration, social platforms and user-generated content.", null, "web2"], ["Social Web", "The Social Web is the part of the Web focused on people, communities, sharing and online relationships.", "Social networking sites let people create profiles, connect with others, publish updates, comment and share media. Blogs and wikis also support community participation. Social systems use identity, feeds, comments and sharing features.", "User creates profile → connects or follows others → publishes content → community responds through comments, likes and shares.", "A student group shares event updates through a social networking platform.", "Fast communication; community building; easy content distribution.", "Cyberbullying, privacy risks, misinformation and addictive design are concerns.", "Social Web encourages user participation and interaction. Blogs, wikis and social networks are key examples.", null, "social"], ["AJAX", "AJAX is a browser technique for asynchronously exchanging data with a server and updating a page without reloading it completely.", "JavaScript sends a background request, receives data and updates a selected page element. AJAX applications commonly use JSON today, even though XML appears in the full name. It made interactive Web 2.0 interfaces more practical.", "Event occurs → request is sent → server responds → callback processes data → DOM update occurs.", "A social feed loads more posts when the user scrolls.", "Faster-feeling interactions; partial page updates; less complete-page rendering.", "Requires robust error handling; accessibility and browser security restrictions must be considered.", "AJAX = Asynchronous JavaScript and XML; modern applications often exchange JSON.", "<pre>fetch('/posts.json')\\n .then(response =&gt; response.json())\\n .then(posts =&gt; console.log(posts));</pre>", "ajax"], ["RSS", "RSS (Really Simple Syndication) is a web feed format used to publish frequently updated content in a standard form.", "A website publishes an RSS feed, usually XML, with items such as titles, links, descriptions and publication dates. A feed reader checks the feed and presents new items so users do not need to visit every site manually.", "Publisher updates RSS XML → feed reader fetches feed → parses items → displays new posts to subscriber.", "A student subscribes to the department news feed to follow announcements.", "Convenient updates; users choose subscriptions; content can be read in one place.", "Feeds may not include full articles; readers and publishers must keep feeds valid.", "RSS is an XML-based syndication format. A channel contains item entries.", "<pre>&lt;rss version=\"2.0\"&gt;\\n &lt;channel&gt;&lt;title&gt;Campus News&lt;/title&gt;\\n  &lt;item&gt;&lt;title&gt;Exam notice&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/item&gt;\\n &lt;/channel&gt;\\n&lt;/rss&gt;</pre>", "rss"], ["Ruby on Rails", "Ruby on Rails is a server-side web application framework written in Ruby.", "Rails follows conventions that reduce repetitive configuration. It commonly uses the Model-View-Controller pattern: models handle data and business rules, views present information, and controllers coordinate requests. Convention over configuration and database migrations are well-known Rails ideas.", "Browser request → route selects controller action → model reads or updates data → view or response is created → browser receives output.", "A small business builds an online catalogue using Rails.", "Rapid development conventions; organised MVC structure; large ecosystem of libraries.", "Convention learning curve; application performance and scaling still require good design.", "Ruby on Rails is a framework, not a programming language. MVC and convention over configuration are key terms.", null, "rails"], ["Open APIs", "An Open API is a documented interface that allows other developers to access a service under stated terms.", "An API describes endpoints, methods, parameters, authentication and response formats. Public documentation helps external applications integrate with a service. Access may require an API key, have rate limits and be governed by usage terms.", "Developer reads API documentation → sends request to endpoint → service validates request → response is returned in a defined format.", "A campus app uses a map API to show directions to a building.", "Encourages integration and reuse; reduces the need to build every capability from scratch.", "Rate limits, API changes, privacy controls and availability can affect dependent apps.", "API means Application Programming Interface. Open does not always mean unrestricted or free.", "<pre>fetch('https://api.example.com/courses')\\n .then(r =&gt; r.json())\\n .then(data =&gt; console.log(data));</pre>", "api"], ["Web 3.0", "Web 3.0 is a broad vision of a more meaningful, connected and machine-understandable Web; the term is used in different ways.", "In semantic-web teaching, Web 3.0 emphasises structured data, metadata, shared vocabularies and links between information. Some modern discussions also use Web3 to refer to blockchain-based decentralised platforms. These meanings should be distinguished by context.", "Information is described with machine-readable metadata → systems connect related resources → applications can discover and combine meaningful data.", "A semantic knowledge graph links a person, a university and a course through explicit relationships.", "Better data integration; richer discovery and machine processing.", "Standards and vocabularies can be complex; data quality and privacy remain challenges.", "For this unit, relate Web 3.0 to Semantic Web, RDF, linked data and structured information.", null, "web3"], ["Semantic Web", "The Semantic Web aims to make web data more meaningful and easier for software to interpret and connect.", "It uses identifiers, metadata, ontologies and graph-based descriptions to represent things and their relationships. Instead of merely matching words, software can use structured relationships to connect facts across sources.", "Describe resources with identifiers → define relationships with vocabularies → publish linked data → applications query or combine information.", "A learning portal can link a course to its instructor, semester and prerequisite topics.", "Improves data integration and discovery; makes relationships explicit.", "Creating consistent vocabularies is difficult; inaccurate metadata can mislead systems.", "Semantic Web makes data machine-readable. RDF and ontologies are important related concepts.", null, "semantic"], ["Widgets", "A widget is a small reusable interface component that provides a focused feature within a page or application.", "Widgets may display weather, a calendar, a search box, a video or a small data panel. They can be built using HTML, CSS and JavaScript or embedded from another service. Good widgets have clear boundaries and responsive behaviour.", "A page includes a widget → widget loads data or behaviour → user interacts with its controls → widget updates its small area.", "A college homepage includes a calendar widget for upcoming examinations.", "Reusable and focused; easy to place in different page areas.", "Too many widgets can clutter a page; external widgets may affect privacy and performance.", "Widget is a small component that performs a specific task or displays focused information.", null, "widget"], ["Mashups", "A mashup is an application that combines content or functionality from multiple sources into one experience.", "Mashups typically use APIs or embedded resources to mix data from several providers. A mashup might combine a map, public transport data and campus locations. Developers must check source permissions, API limits and data accuracy.", "Obtain data from source A and source B → transform or match the data → present combined information in one interface.", "A campus map combines building locations with bus arrival information.", "Combines useful capabilities; creates richer services from existing sources.", "Dependent APIs may change or fail; licensing, rate limits and privacy must be considered.", "Mashups integrate data or services from multiple sources, often through APIs.", null, "mashup"], ["RDF", "RDF (Resource Description Framework) is a standard data model for describing resources and their relationships.", "RDF represents information as triples: subject, predicate and object. Many triples form a graph. Identifiers such as URIs help systems refer to the same resource and connect data from different websites.", "Create subject-predicate-object statements → group triples into a graph → publish or query linked information.", "A statement can say: Web Technology course — hasSemester — Fifth.", "Simple graph model; supports linked data and data integration.", "Large graphs can be difficult to manage; inconsistent identifiers reduce usefulness.", "RDF triple = subject + predicate + object. RDF is a foundation of Semantic Web data.", "<pre>&lt;course&gt; &lt;hasTitle&gt; \"Web Technology\" .</pre>", "rdf"], ["Information Systems", "A web-based information system collects, processes, stores and presents information through a web interface.", "It may include user forms, application logic, data storage and reporting. Examples include student portals, banking applications, booking platforms and library systems. Such systems must manage information accurately and securely.", "User supplies or requests data → application processes it → information is stored or retrieved → system presents useful output.", "A student portal displays marks, attendance and course registrations.", "Accessible through browsers; centralised updates; supports data sharing and reporting.", "Needs security, availability, privacy and careful data management.", "An information system combines people, processes, data and technology to support tasks or decisions.", null, "infosys"], ["Search Engines", "A search engine helps users locate relevant web pages or other information in a large collection.", "Search engines commonly discover pages by crawling links, build an index of content and rank results for a query. Ranking can consider relevance, quality, freshness and many other signals. Exact ranking systems vary by provider.", "Crawler discovers pages → index stores representations → user enters query → ranking system orders matching results → results are displayed.", "A student searches for explanations of HTTP status codes.", "Finds information quickly; searches large collections; supports discovery.", "Results may include spam or low-quality content; ranking can be opaque or biased.", "Remember crawling, indexing and ranking as the main stages.", null, "search"], ["Recommendation Systems", "A recommendation system suggests content, products or activities that may be useful to a user.", "Systems can use item features, user preferences and interaction patterns. Content-based methods compare item attributes; collaborative filtering uses patterns across users or items; hybrid systems combine methods. Recommendations should be evaluated for relevance and fairness.", "Collect allowed preference signals → compare users or content → rank candidate items → display suggestions → learn from feedback where appropriate.", "A learning portal suggests a revision topic related to lessons the student has completed.", "Helps users discover relevant items; personalises large collections.", "Can create filter bubbles; may reflect biased data; privacy must be protected.", "Content-based uses item features; collaborative filtering uses patterns across users or items.", null, "recommend"], ["Blockchain", "Blockchain is a shared digital ledger in which records are grouped into blocks linked using cryptographic hashes.", "Participants maintain copies of a ledger and follow a consensus process to agree on new records. Linking blocks makes later changes detectable. Some blockchains support smart contracts. Not every data problem needs blockchain; performance, governance and privacy matter.", "Transactions are proposed → network participants validate according to consensus → accepted records form a block → block links to previous block.", "A supply chain records product hand-offs so participants can inspect its history.", "Shared record; tamper-evident history; participants can coordinate without one central writer.", "Can consume resources; throughput and governance vary; public records can create privacy concerns.", "Blockchains use cryptographic hashes and consensus. A blockchain is not automatically anonymous or risk-free.", null, "blockchain"], ["Edge Computing", "Edge computing processes data near where it is created rather than sending every task to a distant cloud data centre.", "A local device or gateway can filter sensor data, detect events and respond quickly. The cloud can still be used for long-term storage or large-scale analysis. Edge and cloud computing often work together.", "Device produces data → nearby edge node processes it → urgent response happens locally → selected data may be sent to cloud services.", "A traffic camera detects congestion locally and sends a summary to a city dashboard.", "Can reduce latency and bandwidth use; supports local decisions.", "Edge devices need maintenance and security; local resources are limited.", "Edge means computation near data sources. It complements cloud computing.", null, "edge"], ["Dark Mode", "Dark mode is a user-interface theme that uses dark surfaces with lighter text and controls.", "A website can provide a theme switch or follow the operating system preference. Designers should preserve sufficient contrast, clear focus indicators and readable text. A user preference can be stored in localStorage.", "User selects theme or system preference is read → CSS theme variables change → interface updates → preference may be remembered.", "A student switches a notes portal to dark mode for late-night reading.", "May be comfortable in low-light conditions; offers user choice.", "Poor contrast harms readability; it does not guarantee lower battery use on every screen.", "Dark mode is a design option. Contrast and accessibility are essential.", "<pre>body.dark {\\n background: #101426;\\n color: #f4f6ff;\\n}</pre>", "dark"]] }, { "title": "Web 4.0 & Future Web", "desc": "Intelligent, connected, decentralised and immersive web experiences.", "topics": [["Web 4.0", "Web 4.0 is a future-oriented vision of a more intelligent, connected and context-aware Web.", "It is commonly associated with smart services, AI-assisted features, personalised information, connected devices and adaptive interactions. Unlike HTML or HTTP, Web 4.0 is not one single technical standard; it describes an evolving direction for web experiences.", "Services combine connected data and user context → intelligent tools help interpret or automate tasks → interfaces adapt to the situation → users receive more relevant interactions.", "A smart learning environment could connect course materials, a calendar and device notifications to help a student plan revision.", "Can make services more relevant and connected; may reduce repetitive work.", "Privacy, security, bias, interoperability and user control are important challenges.", "Web 4.0 is a vision, not a single protocol. Connect it to AI, IoT, personalisation and adaptive services.", null, "web4"], ["Artificial Intelligence", "Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the field of creating computer systems that perform tasks associated with human intelligence, such as pattern recognition, prediction or language processing.", "Web applications can use AI services to classify information, detect patterns, answer questions or recommend material. AI is not automatically accurate; outputs need testing and human oversight, especially in education, health and other sensitive settings.", "Data is provided → a model processes patterns learned during training → output or prediction is generated → application presents it for review.", "A learning website groups practice questions by subject or suggests explanations for difficult terms.", "Can automate repetitive analysis; supports pattern-based assistance and discovery.", "Can make mistakes or reflect bias; privacy and transparency require care.", "AI is a broad field. Machine learning is one approach within AI. Always consider data quality and human review.", null, "ai"], ["Personalization", "Personalization adapts information, recommendations or interface settings to a user's needs or preferences.", "A website may use language, selected interests, accessibility preferences or learning choices to tailor its content. Personalisation can be rule-based and does not always require AI. Users should understand and control how their information is used.", "User selects preferences or provides permitted signals → application applies rules or models → relevant layout or content is shown → user can change settings.", "A course portal remembers that a student prefers larger text and recommends the next topic they selected to study.", "Makes content more relevant; can improve usability and discovery.", "Over-personalisation can limit discovery; data collection creates privacy risks.", "Personalisation means adapting experience to a user. It can be preference-based or algorithmic.", null, "personal"], ["Decentralized Web", "The Decentralized Web (DWeb) is an approach to web services that distributes storage, control or communication across multiple participants.", "Rather than relying entirely on one central provider, DWeb systems may use peer-to-peer networking, distributed storage and cryptographic identity. Decentralisation can improve resilience or user control, but coordination and governance remain difficult.", "A user connects to a distributed network → peers locate or exchange data → content may be retrieved from multiple participants → identity and integrity rules are applied.", "Peer-to-peer file sharing distributes files across participants instead of relying on one download server.", "Can reduce dependence on a single provider; may improve resilience and user control.", "Availability, moderation, performance, governance and usability can be challenging.", "DWeb is a design approach. It is not identical to every blockchain or Web3 project.", null, "dweb"], ["Blockchain", "Blockchain is a distributed ledger whose records are grouped into cryptographically linked blocks and updated through a consensus process.", "A web application may read ledger records or submit transactions to a blockchain network. Some networks support smart contracts, which execute programmed rules. Developers must protect private keys and explain transaction fees and irreversibility.", "Application prepares transaction → user or service signs it → network validates it → accepted transaction is recorded → application checks confirmation.", "A certificate-verification service can let a user check whether a certificate record exists on a ledger.", "Shared verification; tamper-evident history; programmable rules on supported networks.", "Transaction costs, throughput, privacy, governance and key security are concerns.", "Blockchain uses blocks, hashes and consensus. Never expose private keys in client-side code.", null, "chain"], ["Internet of Things", "The Internet of Things (IoT) connects physical objects fitted with sensors or software so they can collect and exchange data.", "IoT devices may measure temperature, motion, location or energy use. Devices send readings through a network, sometimes via a gateway, to an application or cloud platform. A web dashboard can display readings and alerts.", "Sensor measures physical condition → device sends data → gateway or service processes it → dashboard displays status or sends an alert.", "Smart classroom sensors report temperature and room occupancy to a web dashboard.", "Remote monitoring; automation; real-time visibility into physical environments.", "Device security, updates, connectivity, interoperability and privacy need attention.", "IoT combines sensors/devices, connectivity, processing and applications.", "<svg viewBox='0 0 600 160' class='diagram'><rect x='10' y='44' width='130' height='65' rx='9'/><rect x='235' y='44' width='130' height='65' rx='9'/><rect x='460' y='44' width='130' height='65' rx='9'/><g><text x='75' y='82'>Sensor</text><text x='300' y='82'>Gateway / Cloud</text><text x='525' y='82'>Web Dashboard</text></g><path d='M140 77 H230 M365 77 H455' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2' fill='none'/><path d='m222 70 8 7-8 7 M447 70 455 77 447 84' stroke='currentColor' fill='none' stroke-width='2'/></svg>", "iot"], ["Augmented Reality", "Augmented Reality (AR) overlays digital information or objects onto a view of the real world.", "AR can place labels, 3D models or instructions over a camera view. Web-based AR experiences may run in compatible browsers and devices. The experience depends on camera permissions, device support, tracking quality and performance.", "Device captures surroundings → software estimates position or surfaces → digital content is placed in the view → user moves and interacts.", "A student points a phone at a diagram and views a 3D model layered over it.", "Can make learning visual and interactive; connects digital information to physical surroundings.", "Needs suitable hardware and tracking; camera permissions, accessibility and privacy matter.", "AR adds digital elements to the real world. It differs from VR, which creates a virtual environment.", null, "ar"], ["Virtual Reality", "Virtual Reality (VR) immerses a user in a computer-generated environment.", "VR uses a headset or screen to display a virtual scene. Head tracking and controllers can allow a user to look around and interact with objects. Web-based VR can deliver experiences through compatible browsers and devices.", "Application renders a 3D scene → headset or display presents it → tracking updates the user's viewpoint → interaction changes the scene.", "A virtual laboratory lets students explore a simulated computer hardware room.", "Immersive practice; can simulate places or tasks that are costly or unsafe to access.", "Hardware can be expensive; motion sickness and accessibility concerns exist.", "VR creates an immersive virtual environment. AR overlays digital objects onto the real world.", null, "vr"], ["Smart Web", "The Smart Web is a broad idea of web services that use connected information and intelligent features to provide useful responses.", "A smart web experience may connect data sources, understand context and automate a suitable action. It often overlaps with semantic data, AI, IoT and personalised services. A smart system should explain important actions and allow user control.", "Gather permitted context → connect or interpret information → provide a useful result or action → allow feedback and correction.", "A campus portal combines room schedules and live occupancy data to help students find an available study space.", "Can connect information across services; may make tasks more convenient.", "Data quality, integration, privacy and system reliability are challenges.", "Smart Web is a broad concept, not one official protocol or fixed technology.", null, "smart"], ["Adaptive Interfaces", "An adaptive interface changes its layout or behaviour to suit a device, user preference or context.", "A responsive interface adjusts to screen size using CSS layout rules. An adaptive interface may also use settings such as preferred text size, language or input method. Adaptation should be predictable and users should be able to override it.", "Detect supported screen or preference → select appropriate layout or settings → render interface → keep controls usable as context changes.", "A learning site switches from a multi-column desktop layout to a one-column phone layout and respects larger text.", "Improves usability on different devices; can support accessibility preferences.", "Unexpected changes can confuse users; testing across devices and settings is needed.", "Responsive design adapts layout to viewport. Adaptive interfaces can also respond to other user or context preferences.", "<pre>@media (max-width: 700px) {\\n  .layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }\\n}</pre>", "adaptive"], ["Automation", "Web automation uses software to perform repeatable browser or website tasks with limited manual effort.", "Automation can test forms, check links, prepare reports, enter routine information or monitor a workflow. It should respect website rules and user permissions. Sensitive actions should include validation and human oversight.", "Define task → run a repeatable script → check conditions and errors → record result → notify or request human review where needed.", "A browser test automatically opens a course page and checks that the heading is visible.", "Reduces repetitive work; improves consistency; supports repeatable testing.", "Poor scripts can repeat errors; credentials and permissions must be protected.", "Automation is useful for testing and routine tasks. It should not bypass access controls or user consent.", null, "automation"], ["5G Technology", "5G is the fifth generation of mobile network technology, designed to support high data rates, lower latency and many connected devices.", "5G can support mobile broadband, IoT, connected transport and immersive media. Actual performance depends on radio coverage, spectrum, network load, device capability and operator deployment.", "Device connects to a 5G radio network → base station connects to operator core → data travels to Internet services or edge infrastructure → application receives response.", "A supported phone uses 5G to stream a high-resolution virtual learning experience.", "Can provide high bandwidth; designed for low latency and dense device connections.", "Coverage and performance vary; infrastructure and device costs can be high.", "5G is a mobile network generation. Do not assume every 5G connection always delivers the same speed or latency.", null, "5g"]] }]; window.WEBVERSE_QUIZZES = [[["Which system translates a domain name into an IP address?", "DNS", "SMTP", "POP3", "HTTP", 0, "DNS resolves domain names using distributed records."], ["Which protocol is mainly used to send email?", "IMAP", "SMTP", "POP3", "FTP", 1, "SMTP submits and relays outgoing email."], ["Which protocol synchronises mailbox folders across devices?", "POP3", "HTTP", "IMAP", "MIME", 2, "IMAP keeps mailbox state synchronised with the server."], ["What does HTTP use to communicate?", "Request-response", "Token ring", "Broadcast only", "File locking", 0, "HTTP follows a request-response model."], ["Which status code usually means Not Found?", "200", "301", "404", "500", 2, "404 indicates that the requested resource was not found."], ["What is MIME used for?", "Email media types and attachments", "IP routing", "Domain lookup", "Page layout", 0, "MIME supports different content types and attachments."], ["What is the Web?", "A computer operating system", "Interlinked resources accessed over the Internet", "A mail protocol", "A cable type", 1, "The Web is a service running on the Internet."], ["Which is a web browser?", "DNS", "Chrome", "SMTP", "TCP", 1, "Chrome is a web browser."], ["What is a web server's main role?", "Return web resources to clients", "Create IP addresses", "Write email", "Render local CSS only", 0, "A web server responds to browser requests."], ["Which record identifies mail servers for a domain?", "A", "CNAME", "MX", "AAAA", 2, "MX records specify mail exchange servers."]], [["Which language structures web content?", "CSS", "HTML", "SQL", "XML Schema", 1, "HTML defines the structure of web pages."], ["Which tag creates an ordered list?", "ul", "li", "ol", "dl", 2, "ol creates a numbered list."], ["Which tag defines a table header cell?", "td", "th", "tr", "thead", 1, "th marks a header cell."], ["Which attribute provides alternative text for an image?", "src", "href", "alt", "title only", 2, "alt conveys image meaning when it cannot be seen."], ["Which form attribute identifies the submitted control key?", "name", "color", "size", "target", 0, "The name is used in submitted name-value pairs."], ["Which older frame elements are obsolete in HTML5?", "iframe", "frameset and frame", "div and span", "section and article", 1, "frameset and frame are obsolete; iframe remains supported."], ["Which technology adds behaviour to a webpage?", "JavaScript", "DTD", "DNS", "SMTP", 0, "JavaScript handles logic and interactions."], ["DHTML combines HTML, CSS, JavaScript and which interface?", "DNS", "DOM", "SMTP", "POP3", 1, "DHTML uses the DOM to manipulate page content."], ["What does CSS control?", "Email delivery", "Page presentation", "Domain lookup", "Database storage", 1, "CSS controls colour, typography, spacing and layout."], ["Which model streams XML parser events?", "DOM", "SAX", "DTD", "XSD", 1, "SAX is event-driven and sequential."]], [["What is a web service?", "A monitor", "A network-accessible software function", "A CSS selector", "A browser extension", 1, "Web services let applications communicate over a network."], ["What does UDDI support?", "Service discovery", "Page styling", "Email attachments", "Image editing", 0, "UDDI is a registry approach to finding services."], ["SOAP messages are based on which format?", "CSV", "XML", "JPEG", "CSS", 1, "SOAP uses structured XML messages."], ["What does WSDL describe?", "A service interface", "A network cable", "A browser theme", "An image format", 0, "WSDL defines operations, messages, bindings and endpoints."], ["Who provides a web service?", "Consumer", "Provider", "Browser cache", "DNS resolver", 1, "The provider hosts and exposes the service."], ["What is an endpoint?", "A service address", "A CSS property", "A list item", "A browser tab", 0, "Clients send requests to a service endpoint."], ["AJAX allows what?", "Background communication and partial page updates", "Only static pages", "Only email sending", "Only image compression", 0, "AJAX updates selected page content without a full reload."], ["Which API is commonly used for modern AJAX requests?", "Fetch", "MIME", "WSDL", "DTD", 0, "The browser Fetch API performs asynchronous requests."], ["What does CORBA use to manage remote object requests?", "ORB", "RSS", "MX", "CSS", 0, "An Object Request Broker manages distributed object calls."], ["Which CORBA language describes interfaces?", "IDL", "HTML", "SQL", "CSS", 0, "IDL is the Interface Definition Language."]], [["Web 2.0 emphasises what?", "User participation", "Static-only sites", "No interaction", "Only email", 0, "Web 2.0 is associated with user-generated content and collaboration."], ["What does RSS distribute?", "Content updates", "IP addresses", "Passwords", "CSS themes", 0, "RSS publishes updates in feed format."], ["Ruby on Rails is written in which language?", "Python", "Ruby", "Java", "C", 1, "Rails is a web framework written in Ruby."], ["An Open API provides what?", "Documented programmatic access", "A private network cable", "An image editor", "A mail client", 0, "An API exposes documented operations for other applications."], ["Semantic Web focuses on what?", "Machine-readable meaning", "Only page colours", "Only video", "Email storage", 0, "Semantic Web structures data and relationships."], ["A widget is best described as what?", "A small reusable interface component", "An IP address", "A mail protocol", "A server room", 0, "Widgets provide a focused feature within a page."], ["A mashup combines what?", "Services or data from multiple sources", "Only CSS selectors", "Only one static image", "Only browser history", 0, "Mashups combine resources into a new experience."], ["An RDF statement contains what?", "Subject, predicate, object", "Header, footer, body", "GET, POST, PUT", "Name, password, token", 0, "RDF uses subject-predicate-object triples."], ["What are the common search engine stages?", "Crawl, index, rank", "Send, receive, delete", "Parse, paint, print", "Encrypt, compress, upload", 0, "Search engines discover pages, index them and rank results."], ["Edge computing processes data where?", "Near the data source", "Only in a distant data centre", "Only in a browser cache", "Only in DNS", 0, "Edge nodes are placed closer to devices or users."]], [["Web 4.0 is commonly presented as what?", "A future intelligent and connected web vision", "A markup tag", "A mail server", "A browser version", 0, "Web 4.0 is a vision of intelligent, context-aware services."], ["AI stands for what?", "Automated Internet", "Artificial Intelligence", "Application Interface", "Adaptive Integration", 1, "AI is Artificial Intelligence."], ["Personalisation adapts a service to what?", "User preferences or needs", "Only domain names", "Only IP packets", "Only server hardware", 0, "Personalisation tailors content or interface to users."], ["DWeb refers to what?", "Decentralized Web", "Dark Web only", "Document Web", "Dynamic Web Elements", 0, "DWeb distributes aspects of storage, control or communication."], ["Blockchain records are linked using what?", "Cryptographic hashes", "CSS classes", "HTML tables", "Mail headers", 0, "Cryptographic hashes help link blocks."], ["IoT connects what?", "Physical devices and sensors", "Only browsers", "Only email servers", "Only text files", 0, "IoT connects physical devices to networks and services."], ["AR does what?", "Overlays digital content on the real world", "Creates only a virtual world", "Sends email", "Indexes websites", 0, "AR augments a real-world view."], ["VR does what?", "Creates an immersive virtual environment", "Translates domain names", "Styles headings", "Sends email", 0, "VR places users in a computer-generated environment."], ["An adaptive interface can respond to what?", "Screen size or user preferences", "Only email headers", "Only domain records", "Only file extensions", 0, "Adaptive interfaces adjust to device, user or context."], ["5G is which generation of mobile networks?", "Third", "Fourth", "Fifth", "Sixth", 2, "5G is the fifth generation."]]];